{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And the width fifty by fifty.</b> Not fifty precisely, but fifty from north to south and forty-nine from east to the Mishkan. How so? We have it as a tradition: the length of the courtyard is one hundred cubits and its width fifty, and the length of the Mishkan together with the thickness of the western planks and the thickness of the sockets on the east is thirty-two cubits, and its width twelve cubits together with the thickness of the planks. They placed the Mishkan lengthwise along the length of the courtyard and in the middle of the forty-nine cubits, toward the screen of the gate of the courtyard — it turns out that the courtyard gate on the east is fifty by forty-nine, and that on the west fifty cubits by nineteen, and that on the north and south one hundred by nineteen." "<b>And its height five cubits of twisted linen, and their sockets copper.</b> According to the plain sense, this is what it says: the <b>height</b> of the courtyard partitions — <b>five cubits</b> from the hangings, which were of twisted linen, apart from the copper sockets. It turns out that the height of the partitions was six cubits, for unspecified sockets are one cubit high. And in tractate Zevachim it is explained thus: the width of the hangings, which is their height, was fifteen (cubits), and how do I fulfill 'and its height five cubits'? From the altar and above — this follows Rabbi Yossi, who says its height was twice its width."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:00:47.641265758Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}